WEBVTT

1
00:00:15.307 --> 00:00:19.389
I accompanied Holmes to the village inn, and we arranged for a room from which we could see

2
00:00:19.469 --> 00:00:21.250
Julia's window at Stoke Moran.

3
00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:26.293
While we waited for the lantern signal, Holmes asked me to assist him with a review of the

4
00:00:26.353 --> 00:00:32.296
facts. "Well, there seemed to be nothing strange about Miss Stoner's room," I began.

5
00:00:33.024 --> 00:00:35.625
"But her sister Julia's room did seem a bit odd.

6
00:00:36.345 --> 00:00:40.526
First of all, the bed was bolted to the floor, which prevented it from being moved.

7
00:00:41.366 --> 00:00:45.047
Then, of course, there was the bell rope—it didn't even work!

8
00:00:45.827 --> 00:00:49.808
And, from what Miss Stoner has told us, there would be no use for it anyway.

9
00:00:50.629 --> 00:00:54.190
Also, there was the ventilation window that linked her room to Dr.

10
00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:58.211
Roylott's." "I think that's everything, Watson," said Holmes.

11
00:00:58.231 --> 00:01:01.031
"Now, what about Roylott's room?"

12
00:01:01.051 --> 00:01:07.455
"Two things are puzzling," I replied, "the saucer of milk on the floor and the dog's leash hanging

13
00:01:07.495 --> 00:01:08.316
from the bedpost."

14
00:01:08.336 --> 00:01:11.618
"Indeed," said Holmes.

15
00:01:12.399 --> 00:01:17.082
For several hours I sat in the armchair by the window, keeping watch for the signal as Holmes

16
00:01:17.122 --> 00:01:19.624
reclined on the bed with his hat tipped over his eyes.

17
00:01:20.484 --> 00:01:22.065
Finally he broke the silence.

18
00:01:22.886 --> 00:01:26.749
"It's going to be a dangerous night, my dear Watson," he said.

19
00:01:26.769 --> 00:01:31.132
"If you wish to return to the safety of London, I will not hold it against you."

20
00:01:32.028 --> 00:01:33.269
"I will be at your side, Holmes!"

21
00:01:33.849 --> 00:01:34.830
I quickly responded.

22
00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:38.472
"I only wish that I could deduce more from the clues.

23
00:01:39.312 --> 00:01:42.274
I hate to admit it, but I don't really know what's happening."

24
00:01:43.015 --> 00:01:44.596
Holmes gave me a kind smile.

25
00:01:44.616 --> 00:01:51.920
"Watson," he said, "you are a trustworthy man, a fine friend, and an excellent associate.

26
00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:54.161
I knew I could count on you."

27
00:01:54.922 --> 00:01:58.284
Just after midnight I spotted the lantern and alerted Holmes.

28
00:01:58.992 --> 00:02:03.394
As he prepared his things, I slipped my pistol into the inside pocket of my suit jacket, and

29
00:02:03.434 --> 00:02:04.234
we left the inn.

30
00:02:05.375 --> 00:02:10.537
We walked along the pathway toward the window of Julia's room, which had been left open for us.

31
00:02:11.457 --> 00:02:15.499
I was just about to give Holmes a boost up to the window when I noticed a hideous

32
00:02:15.599 --> 00:02:16.920
figure moving in the shadows.

33
00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:18.200
"Oh my!

34
00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:19.240
What was that?"

35
00:02:19.961 --> 00:02:22.182
I whispered, trying hard not to appear frightened.

36
00:02:22.202 --> 00:02:23.922
"It's the baboon!"

37
00:02:24.463 --> 00:02:25.163
replied Holmes.

38
00:02:25.183 --> 00:02:27.504
"It gave me quite a fright as well."

39
00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:30.061
We looked at each other and laughed softly.

40
00:02:30.782 --> 00:02:33.663
Then, quietly, we climbed through the window and into the room.

41
00:02:33.683 --> 00:02:38.186
"It may be a while before anything occurs," advised Holmes.

42
00:02:38.206 --> 00:02:41.127
"Why don't you sit in the chair and get comfortable?"

43
00:02:41.808 --> 00:02:47.070
Without an explanation Holmes then placed a pack of matches next to the candles on the bedside table

44
00:02:47.591 --> 00:02:50.953
and lay down on the bed, holding his cane close to his side.

45
00:02:51.673 --> 00:02:56.616
I barely had a chance to ponder his actions before a gleam of light appeared through the ventilation

46
00:02:56.656 --> 00:03:01.587
window. This was accompanied by the smell of burning oil and heated metal.

47
00:03:02.427 --> 00:03:05.229
"Roylott has lit his lantern," Holmes whispered.

48
00:03:05.910 --> 00:03:06.851
"Be prepared."

49
00:03:07.631 --> 00:03:10.053
There was no further action for another few minutes.

50
00:03:10.753 --> 00:03:15.277
Then the silence was interrupted by an odd hissing sound much like that of a jet of steam

51
00:03:15.357 --> 00:03:16.438
escaping from a kettle.

52
00:03:17.158 --> 00:03:22.482
When Holmes heard it, he immediately leaped from the bed, lit the candles, and then lashed the bed

53
00:03:22.522 --> 00:03:23.163
with his cane.

54
00:03:23.983 --> 00:03:28.333
My heart was racing, but with all the shadows, I couldn't see what was happening.

55
00:03:29.174 --> 00:03:31.354
I could tell, however, that Holmes was frightened.

56
00:03:31.915 --> 00:03:33.775
All the color had drained from his face.

57
00:03:34.436 --> 00:03:38.537
Just then a piercing scream came from Roylott's room, and Holmes stopped.

58
00:03:38.557 --> 00:03:39.898
"Is it over?"

59
00:03:40.578 --> 00:03:41.438
I asked frantically.

60
00:03:41.458 --> 00:03:44.240
"I believe so," responded Holmes.

61
00:03:44.260 --> 00:03:46.240
"Follow me to the doctor's room."

62
00:03:47.021 --> 00:03:50.682
We rushed toward Roylott's door, which luckily we discovered unlocked.

63
00:03:51.502 --> 00:03:55.004
Inside I was shocked to see the doctor lying face up on the floor.

64
00:03:56.288 --> 00:04:00.051
His face was frozen in a look of absolute terror, and he was clearly dead.

65
00:04:01.012 --> 00:04:05.795
Around his head was a speckled band—was this what had killed Julia too?

66
00:04:06.796 --> 00:04:10.298
I was about to lean down to examine it when Holmes suddenly pulled me away.

67
00:04:11.099 --> 00:04:13.300
Much to my surprise, the band moved!

68
00:04:13.761 --> 00:04:16.903
It was then that I realized the reason for Holmes' action.

69
00:04:17.644 --> 00:04:20.806
It was a swamp adder, the most poisonous snake in India!

70
00:04:21.566 --> 00:04:27.275
Holmes quickly stepped over the body, grabbed the leash from the bedpost, and snapped the looped end around

71
00:04:27.315 --> 00:04:28.076
the snake's neck.

72
00:04:28.977 --> 00:04:34.061
Then he threw the serpent into the filing cabinet and closed the drawer, securing the snake inside.

73
00:04:34.882 --> 00:04:36.183
I gave a sigh of relief.

74
00:04:36.203 --> 00:04:38.906
"Don't worry, Watson," Holmes said.

75
00:04:38.926 --> 00:04:42.729
"Go get Miss Stoner and let her know that we have solved her mystery."

76
00:04:43.390 --> 00:04:48.054
The next morning, after the police had been called and the two of us had given our testimony,

77
00:04:48.454 --> 00:04:50.036
we caught the train home to London.

78
00:04:51.248 --> 00:04:55.430
I took this opportunity to ask Holmes to tell me how he had known about the snake.

79
00:04:55.450 --> 00:04:57.471
"Ah," he began.

80
00:04:57.491 --> 00:05:01.512
"I was initially confused by Miss Stoner's mention of the Gypsies.

81
00:05:02.333 --> 00:05:07.115
But the more I began to research the case, the more it seemed unfair to blame the crime

82
00:05:07.195 --> 00:05:07.635
on them.

83
00:05:08.275 --> 00:05:09.456
It was obvious that Dr.

84
00:05:09.496 --> 00:05:12.157
Roylott had the only motive for committing the murder.

85
00:05:12.897 --> 00:05:18.099
At the hospital library, I learned that snake venom might also cause its victim to die without any

86
00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:22.534
visible sign—I have always been fascinated by toxicology!

87
00:05:23.515 --> 00:05:28.958
I suspected a snake could be the culprit after Miss Stoner mentioned Roylott's love of exotic animals.

88
00:05:29.779 --> 00:05:36.444
Of course, when we discovered the ventilation window that connected Roylott's room to Julia's, I was convinced a

89
00:05:36.524 --> 00:05:37.885
snake was the murder weapon.

90
00:05:38.705 --> 00:05:43.249
I knew that serpents do not like fire, which is why I brought the pack of matches with

91
00:05:43.329 --> 00:05:44.950
us to the room and lit the candle."

92
00:05:47.756 --> 00:05:47.996
"I see!"

93
00:05:48.016 --> 00:05:48.276
I exclaimed.

94
00:05:48.296 --> 00:05:49.437
"But what about the other clues?

95
00:05:49.797 --> 00:05:50.997
The bell rope, for example?"

96
00:05:51.638 --> 00:05:55.459
"The bell rope worked like a bridge between the ventilation window and the bed.

97
00:05:56.139 --> 00:06:00.821
Roylott must have trained the snake to climb through the ventilation window and down the bell rope onto

98
00:06:00.861 --> 00:06:03.943
the bed, which, you will recall, was bolted to the floor.

99
00:06:04.723 --> 00:06:10.745
The snake must have done this for several nights without attacking, but eventually it did and poor Julia

100
00:06:10.785 --> 00:06:11.266
was bitten."

101
00:06:11.286 --> 00:06:15.807
"How do you explain the whistling sound and the metal clang that Miss Stoner reported?"

102
00:06:16.428 --> 00:06:16.848
I asked.

103
00:06:17.504 --> 00:06:19.065
"We didn't hear either last night."

104
00:06:19.585 --> 00:06:22.546
Holmes gave me a big smile and said, "Aha!

105
00:06:23.106 --> 00:06:27.088
I believe that the doctor had trained the snake to return to his room when it heard the

106
00:06:27.128 --> 00:06:30.609
whistle—just as a snake charmer would control a cobra.

107
00:06:31.190 --> 00:06:33.311
The snake was rewarded each time."

108
00:06:33.331 --> 00:06:35.331
"With the saucer of milk?"

109
00:06:35.912 --> 00:06:38.073
I asked, suddenly understanding another clue.

110
00:06:38.673 --> 00:06:42.414
"Precisely. Now, remember the chair in Roylott's room?

111
00:06:43.075 --> 00:06:45.936
He must have stood on it to reach the ventilation window.

112
00:06:46.772 --> 00:06:52.054
The clang was likely the sound of the metal window being closed after the deed had been done.

113
00:06:52.795 --> 00:06:58.297
However, last night, when I frightened the snake with the lit match, it retreated back up the bell

114
00:06:58.357 --> 00:07:01.498
rope and through the ventilation window before Roylott expected.

115
00:07:02.138 --> 00:07:05.720
Not only that, but I had made the snake quite angry with my cane.

116
00:07:06.460 --> 00:07:10.342
It attacked the first person it found—its master, Dr.

117
00:07:10.442 --> 00:07:18.135
Roylott!" "Holmes," I replied, "if what you are saying is true, then you are responsible for the doctor's

118
00:07:18.175 --> 00:07:26.284
death." Holmes just laughed and said, "I suppose that is correct, but it is not a death that

119
00:07:26.344 --> 00:07:27.565
I will ever regret."
